// NOTE: This module is non-standard!
/// Tiling and layering: covering a surface with several sources without
/// showing where one stops and the next begins.
///
/// There are two kinds of seam to hide and one vocabulary that hides both.
/// Hex tiling breaks up the repetition *within* one periodic texture by
/// covering the UV plane with randomly offset and rotated hexagonal tiles;
/// layering decides which of several sources covers a shading point at all,
/// from painted coverage, terrain slope, altitude, noise, or anything else
/// the material computes. Both come down to a set of weights that sum to one
/// and a decision about how to resolve them, so the weights are one tier
/// shared between them:
///
///   1. `layer_slope()`, `layer_altitude()`, `layer_mask()` and
///      `layer_band()` build coverage from the shading state.
///   2. `weight_normalize()`, `weight_modulate()`, `weight_contrast()` and
///      `weight_compete()` shape a set of weights, and `weight_blend()`,
///      `weight_select()` and `weight_select_remap()` resolve one.
///   3. `hex_lattice_at()` builds the hex lattice, which is one more source
///      of weights: three tiles covering the point, each with the
///      coordinate to look a source up at.
///
/// Prefer the stochastic path. `hex_tile_at()` returns a single tile chosen
/// with probability equal to its weight, and every map is then read with an
/// ordinary `tex::lookup_*()` at `tile.coord`. One tiled texture:
///
///     const auto tile(hex_tile_at(coord));
///     const auto base(tex::lookup_color(color_tex, tile.coord));
///
/// and a normal map beside it, which has to come from the same tile and
/// back through that tile's own rotation:
///
///     const auto normal(hex_lookup_normal(normal_tex, tile));
///
/// That is one texture fetch per map rather than three, it hashes and
/// rotates one tile rather than three, and every sample is a value that
/// genuinely occurs in the source, so the histogram is preserved exactly
/// rather than approximated. Averaged over the samples a Monte Carlo
/// renderer already takes, it reproduces the three-tap blend. It is also the
/// correct thing whenever the value feeds a non-linearity: shading a blend of
/// three roughnesses is not the same as blending three shaded roughnesses.
/// The random number defaults to `state::random_float()`, which the renderer
/// is expected to seed per shading point; pass `xi` explicitly to chain one
/// number through several choices, or to keep the material deterministic.
///
/// Layers compose with tiles without either knowing about the other. Shape
/// the coverage, choose a layer, and tile that layer alone:
///
///     const auto w(weight_normalize(cover));
///     const auto k(weight_select(w, state::random_float()));
///     const auto tile(hex_tile_at(coord * scale[k], seed: k));
///
/// so that the cost does not grow with the number of layers. The exception
/// is `weight_compete()`, the height competition that makes a boundary
/// follow the texture content instead of the smooth coverage mask: it must
/// see every layer's height, so it costs a tiling and a fetch per layer. It
/// is the one thing in a layered material that is not paid for once, and on
/// a four-layer material it is about a third of the total.
///
/// What things cost, in units of one bilinear fetch, is worth knowing before
/// reaching for the deterministic path: a hex tiling is about two fetches, and
/// so is the RGB-to-spectrum conversion every `color` from a texture goes
/// through. Blend in `float3` or `float4` and convert once at the end;
/// converting per tile or per layer is the most expensive mistake available
/// here.
///
/// The deterministic three-tap path is still here for the cases the
/// stochastic one cannot serve, being anything read outside a Monte Carlo
/// estimator: displacement, cutout opacity, and hosts that leave
/// `state::random_float()` fixed. Build a lattice with `hex_lattice_at()`,
/// look each map up against it with `hex_lookup()`, and combine with
/// `hex_blend()` or `hex_blend_variance_preserving()`. Only this path can
/// modulate the weights by content, since `weight_modulate()` needs all
/// three values, so the two agree in expectation only when it does not.
///
/// Default rule: shaping primitives default to their identity so they
/// compose neutrally (`weight_contrast()` at 0.5), while the lattice and the
/// turnkey lookups default to the recommended configuration (`rotate` 1.0,
/// `contrast` 0.75, `strength` 0.6).
///
/// The technique assumes a stationary source. A texture with strong
/// low-frequency content is the wrong thing to hex tile: every tile samples
/// a different part of that slow drift, and the lattice shows up as a
/// patchwork of tones that no amount of weight shaping will hide.
///
/// No mip-mapping or screen-space derivative is required or used: under
/// minification the result aliases, and the renderer's per-pixel Monte Carlo
/// sampling resolves it to the correct answer without prefiltering.
///
/// The lattice departs from the published implementation twice, both times
/// for numerical conditioning rather than method: the lookup coordinate is
/// derived from the fractional part of the skewed coordinate instead of by
/// subtracting the tile center from the position, which cancels
/// catastrophically at large coordinates, and the skew and floor are
/// evaluated in double so the tile ids stay consistent at any magnitude.
/// Together these hold the lookup coordinate to float epsilon everywhere.
///
/// NOTE: The weight vocabulary is generic in the container, so one set of
/// functions shapes the three weights the lattice carries as a `float3` and
/// the N layer weights a material carries as a `float[N]`. Arguments that
/// must agree in length say so with an assertion, which is decided at
/// compile time.
///
/// References:
///   Mikkelsen (2022): Practical Real-Time Hex-Tiling
///   Heitz and Neyret (2018): High-performance by-example noise
///   Burley (2019): On histogram-preserving blending
///   Perlin and Hoffert (1989): Hypertexture
///   Mishkinis (2013): Advanced Terrain Texture Splatting
///   Pharr, Jakob, Humphreys (2023): Physically Based Rendering, 4th ed.
#smdl

import ::extras::noise::*;
import ::math::*;
import ::state::*;
import ::tex::*;

//--{ Masks

/// The slope of the shading normal in radians relative to `up`, which must
/// be unit length: `0` on flat ground, `pi / 2` on a vertical wall, beyond
/// `pi / 2` on an overhang.
@(macro)
export float layer_slope(const float3 up = float3(0.0, 0.0, 1.0)) {
  const auto normal(math::normalize(state::transform_normal(
    state::coordinate_internal, state::coordinate_world, state::normal())));
  return #acos(math::clamp(math::dot(normal, up), -1.0, 1.0));
}

/// The world-space height of the shading position along `up`, which must be
/// unit length.
@(macro)
export float layer_altitude(const float3 up = float3(0.0, 0.0, 1.0)) = //
math::dot(
  state::transform_point(
    state::coordinate_internal, state::coordinate_world, state::position()),
  up);

/// The smooth mask that rises from zero to one as `x` crosses from `edge0`
/// to `edge1`, and holds at one thereafter. Reversing the edges yields the
/// falling mask. This is `math::smoothstep()` with the value first, in the
/// reading order the rest of the `layer_*` family uses.
@(pure macro)
export float layer_mask(const float x, const float edge0, const float edge1) = //
math::smoothstep(edge0, edge1, x);

/// The smooth band mask that rises from zero to one over `rise` and falls
/// back to zero over `fall`, for carving coverage out of a scalar field:
/// snow between two altitudes, grass below a slope angle, and so on.
@(pure macro)
export float layer_band(const float x, const float2 rise, const float2 fall) = //
layer_mask(x, rise.x, rise.y) * layer_mask(x, fall.y, fall.x);

//--}

//--{ Weights

/// Normalizes blending weights to be non-negative and sum to one, falling
/// back to a uniform split if they have all been driven to zero.
///
/// The weight vocabulary is generic in the container, so the same functions
/// shape the three weights the hex lattice carries as a `float3` and the N
/// layer weights a material carries as a `float[N]`.
@(pure macro)
export auto weight_normalize(auto w) {
  const auto n(#num(w));
  float sum(0.0);
  for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    w[i] = #max(w[i], 0.0);
    sum += w[i];
  }
  if (sum > 1e-20) {
    const auto invSum(1.0 / sum);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
      w[i] *= invSum;
  } else {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
      w[i] = 1.0 / n;
  }
  return w;
}

/// Modulates blending weights by a per-entry significance metric,
/// renormalizing afterward.
///
/// This is the substitute for histogram preservation, and the
/// generalization of it to layers: each weight is scaled by how much its
/// source matters here, so the transition is diffused by the content instead
/// of cutting straight across it. The metric is the caller's choice;
/// luminance is usual for color, and `hex_metric_luminance()` computes it for
/// a set of tile samples. The `strength` is the extent to which the metric
/// matters, so that the effective factor is
/// `1 - strength + strength * metric`; zero ignores it entirely.
@(pure macro)
export auto weight_modulate(
  auto w, const auto metric, const float strength = 0.6) {
  #assert(#num(w) == #num(metric), "metric must have one entry per weight");
  for (int i = 0; i < #num(w); i++)
    w[i] *= 1.0 - strength + strength * #max(metric[i], 0.0);
  return weight_normalize(w);
}

/// Applies Perlin's S-curve ramp to blending weights, renormalizing
/// afterward.
///
/// Pushing the weights toward zero and one wins back some of the contrast
/// that omitting histogram preservation costs, at the price of more apparent
/// boundaries. A `contrast` of 0.5 is exactly the identity, which is why it
/// is the default here even though the lattice defaults to the recommended
/// 0.75: a shaping primitive composes neutrally unless asked otherwise.
/// Above 0.5 sharpens, below softens. The published recommendation is 0.65
/// to 0.75 for color and 0.5, i.e. disabled, for normal maps; the stochastic
/// path can be pushed considerably harder than either, because the sampler
/// dithers a sharp ramp instead of showing it as a seam.
@(pure macro)
export auto weight_contrast(auto w, const float contrast = 0.5) {
  return w if (contrast == 0.5);
  const auto k(#log(#max(1e-4, 1.0 - contrast)) / #log(0.5));
  for (int i = 0; i < #num(w); i++) {
    const auto s(w[i] < 0.5 ? 0.0 : 2.0);
    const auto m(2.0 * (1.0 - s));
    w[i] = 0.5 * s + 0.25 * m * #pow(#max(0.0, s + w[i] * m), k);
  }
  return weight_normalize(w);
}

/// Resolves blending weights by height competition, renormalizing
/// afterward.
///
/// This is the height-based blending of terrain splatting: each entry bids
/// its weighted height `w * height`, and only those within `transition` of
/// the highest bid survive, so at a boundary the taller features of one
/// layer break through the other and the transition follows the texture
/// content instead of the smooth driving mask. It is the hard-edged cousin
/// of `weight_modulate()`: modulation diffuses a transition by content,
/// while competition carves it. The natural `height` for a layer is its
/// height or displacement map read through its own hex tiling, which is the
/// one thing in a layered material that must be read for every layer rather
/// than only the chosen one.
///
/// The bid is the product of weight and height rather than the published
/// sum, so a layer with zero coverage can never appear no matter how tall
/// its texture, and the small floor keeps the plain weights in charge
/// wherever the heights tie or vanish. As `transition` grows the result
/// approaches the unresolved weights; toward zero it approaches
/// winner-take-all.
///
/// NOTE: This is the one place the stochastic and deterministic paths
/// define different looks rather than the same one resolved two ways.
/// Competition is not linear in the heights, so feeding it one chosen
/// tile's height per layer does not converge on feeding it the blend: the
/// boundary it carves follows the lattice as much as the texture, and it
/// needs samples to settle. That is a legitimate look and the cheap one;
/// pass heights from `hex_lookup()` instead when the boundary has to be
/// the deterministic one.
@(pure macro)
export auto weight_compete(
  auto w, const auto height, const float transition = 0.2) {
  #assert(#num(w) == #num(height), "height must have one entry per weight");
  float peak(0.0);
  for (int i = 0; i < #num(w); i++)
    peak = #max(peak, w[i] * #max(height[i], 0.0));
  const auto t(#max(transition, 1e-4));
  for (int i = 0; i < #num(w); i++)
    w[i] *= #max(w[i] * #max(height[i], 0.0) - peak + t, 0.0) + 1e-6 * t;
  return weight_normalize(w);
}

/// The weighted blend of N values of any type.
@(pure macro)
export auto weight_blend(const auto w, const auto values) {
  #assert(#num(w) == #num(values), "values must have one entry per weight");
  auto result(w[0] * values[0]);
  for (int i = 1; i < #num(w); i++)
    result += w[i] * values[i];
  return result;
}

/// The index chosen stochastically, with probability equal to its weight,
/// given a canonical random number `xi` in `[0, 1)` and weights that sum to
/// one, as every function above produces.
///
/// Averaged over many samples this reproduces `weight_blend()` exactly, and
/// under a Monte Carlo renderer it is the unbiased substitute that evaluates
/// one source rather than N. See `hex_tile_at()` for why that is the
/// preferable way to resolve values that feed non-linearities, and
/// `weight_select_remap()` for the form that chains into a further choice.
@(pure macro)
export int weight_select(const auto w, const float xi) {
  float c(0.0);
  for (int i = 0; i < #num(w) - 1; i++) {
    c += w[i];
    return i if (xi < c);
  }
  return #num(w) - 1;
}

/// Evaluates `f` at one index chosen stochastically: the general form of the
/// stochastic resolution, costing a single evaluation of `f` however many
/// entries there are and however each one is computed. The lambda receives
/// the index.
@(macro)
export auto weight_select_with(const auto w, const float xi, const auto f) =
  f(weight_select(w, xi));

/// A stochastic choice: the chosen index together with the random number
/// remapped to be uniform again, so that one canonical random number can
/// drive a chain of choices.
export struct weight_choice {
  /// The index of the chosen entry.
  int index = 0;

  /// The remapped random number, uniform on `[0, 1)` conditioned on the
  /// choice, ready to drive a further choice such as `hex_tile_at()` within
  /// the chosen layer.
  float xi = 0.0;
};

/// The stochastic choice at `xi`, with remapping: conditioned on landing in
/// the chosen entry's interval, the leftover fraction of `xi` is uniform on
/// `[0, 1)` again, so it is safe to reuse for a further choice and one
/// random number buys the whole chain. The reuse correlates the two choices
/// slightly, which decorrelated per-layer lattices render harmless. A
/// material with a renderer-seeded generator may simply draw twice instead,
/// which is simpler and fully decorrelated; this exists for one that plumbs
/// its own `xi`.
@(pure macro)
export weight_choice weight_select_remap(const auto w, const float xi) {
  float c(0.0);
  for (int i = 0; i < #num(w) - 1; i++) {
    return weight_choice(index: i, xi: #min((xi - c) / w[i], 0.99999994)) if (xi < c + w[i]);
    c += w[i];
  }
  return weight_choice(
           index: #num(w) - 1,
           xi: #min((xi - c) / #max(1.0 - c, 1e-20), 0.99999994));
}

//--}

//--{ Hex lattice

/// The distance between adjacent hex tile centers, `1 / (2 sqrt(3))`. This is
/// also the largest distance from a position to any of its three covering
/// tile centers, and so bounds the lookup coordinate that the lattice
/// produces relative to the random per-tile offset.
export const float HEX_TILE_RADIUS = 0.28867513459481287;

/// The scale and skew that carry the UV plane onto the triangle lattice.
const double HEX_SKEW_X = 3.4641016151377544; // 2 sqrt(3)

/// One hexagonal tile covering a shading position.
export struct hex_tile {
  /// The lattice id, which identifies the tile uniquely and is suitable for
  /// seeding further per-tile randomization.
  int2 id = int2(0);

  /// The coordinate at which to look up the source for this tile.
  float2 coord = float2(0.0);

  /// The random rotation applied to the lookup coordinate, as `[cos, sin]`.
  /// Directional values looked up from this tile, such as tangent-space
  /// normals, must come back through its inverse: see `hex_rotate_inverse()`
  /// and `hex_lookup_normal()`.
  float2 rotation = float2(1.0, 0.0);
};

/// The three hex tiles overlapping a shading position.
export struct hex_lattice {
  /// The tiles.
  hex_tile[3] tiles = hex_tile[3](hex_tile(), hex_tile(), hex_tile());

  /// The blending weights, which are non-negative and sum to one.
  ///
  /// These are the barycentric weights of the position among the three tile
  /// centers, sharpened by exponent 7 and normalized: a plain barycentric
  /// blend ghosts badly, and sharpening narrows the transition to a hexagonal
  /// mask, which is Burley's (2019) observation; 7 is the published choice.
  /// Shape these further with `weight_modulate()` and `weight_contrast()`.
  float3 weights = float3(1.0 / 3.0);
};

/// The triangle lattice cell containing a position: everything the three
/// covering tiles have in common, so that the weights can be shaped and one
/// tile chosen before any tile is hashed or rotated.
struct hexCell {
  /// The lattice coordinate of the containing rhombus.
  int2 base = int2(0);

  /// The fractional part of the skewed position within it.
  float2 f = float2(0.0);

  /// Which of the two triangles of the rhombus holds the position, as the
  /// 0 or 1 that `hexDeltaOf()` reads.
  int i = 0;

  /// The raw barycentric weights among the three tile centers, before
  /// sharpening.
  float3 barycentric = float3(1.0 / 3.0);
};

/// The inverse of the skew, carrying the triangle lattice back to the UV plane.
@(pure macro)
float2 hexUnskew(const float2 p) =
  float2(HEX_TILE_RADIUS * (p.x + 0.5 * p.y), 0.25 * p.y);

/// The lattice offset of the tile at index `k`, which its id and its
/// fractional offset are both measured from. Two selects rather than an
/// index into a spilled array.
@(pure macro)
int2 hexDeltaOf(const int i, const int k) =
  k == 0 ? int2(i, i) : (k == 1 ? int2(i, 1 - i) : int2(1 - i, i));

/// The cell containing `coord`.
///
/// The skew into the triangle lattice and the split of the containing
/// rhombus into the two triangles whose corners are the three covering tile
/// centers. Done in double so that the fractional part, and hence the tile
/// ids, stay exact however large the coordinate is.
@(pure macro)
hexCell hexCellAt(const float2 coord) {
  const auto skewed(double2(
    HEX_SKEW_X * double(coord.x) - 2.0d * double(coord.y),
    4.0d * double(coord.y)));
  const auto base(int2(#floor(skewed)));
  const auto f(float2(skewed - double2(base)));
  const auto fz(1.0 - f.x - f.y);
  const auto upper(fz < 0.0);
  const auto s(upper ? 1.0 : 0.0);
  const auto s2(upper ? 1.0 : -1.0);
  return hexCell(
           base: base,
           f: f,
           i: upper ? 1 : 0,
           barycentric: #max(
             float3(0.0), float3(-fz * s2, s - f.y * s2, s - f.x * s2)));
}

/// The published exponent-7 sharpen of the raw barycentric weights,
/// normalized.
@(pure macro)
float3 hexSharpen(const float3 b) {
  const auto b2(b * b);
  return weight_normalize(b2 * b2 * b2 * b);
}

/// The tile of `cell` at index `k`.
///
/// The random offset comes straight out of the fractional part, so it is
/// bounded by `HEX_TILE_RADIUS` and never suffers the cancellation that
/// subtracting the tile center from the position would.
@(pure macro)
hex_tile hexTileOf(
  const hexCell cell, const int k, const float rotate, const int seed) {
  const auto delta(hexDeltaOf(cell.i, k));
  const auto id(cell.base + delta);
  const auto h(::extras::noise::hash_float3(int3(id.x, id.y, seed)));
  auto offset(hexUnskew(cell.f - float2(delta)));
  auto rotation(float2(1.0, 0.0));
  if (rotate != 0.0) {
    const auto angle((2.0 * h.z - 1.0) * ($PI * rotate));
    rotation = float2(#cos(angle), #sin(angle));
    offset = float2(rotation.x * offset.x - rotation.y * offset.y, //
    rotation.y * offset.x + rotation.x * offset.y);
  }
  return hex_tile(id: id, coord: offset + h.xy, rotation: rotation);
}

/// The hex tiling covering `coord`: all three tiles and their weights.
///
/// Each tile is assigned a uniformly random offset, so the source is sampled
/// at an unrelated location per tile and its repetition is broken up. The
/// `rotate` parameter additionally applies a random per-tile rotation, as a
/// fraction of `+/-pi`, which matters when the source has oriented features;
/// zero disables it. Full rotation is the default, since hiding repetition
/// is the point of the technique; pass `rotate: 0.0` for oriented sources
/// such as brick or wood. The `seed` decorrelates independent tilings of the
/// same surface.
///
/// This is the deterministic path: prefer `hex_tile_at()` unless the result
/// is read outside a Monte Carlo estimator. Taking the tiling rather than
/// building one is what lets several maps share it, which is the usual shape
/// of a material: build it once here, then look up each map against it with
/// `hex_lookup()`.
///
/// NOTE: If `rotate` is non-zero, directional values such as tangent-space
/// normals must be counter-rotated after lookup: use `hex_rotate_inverse()`
/// with `tiles[k].rotation`, or `hex_lookup_normal()` for a normal map.
@(pure noinline)
export hex_lattice hex_lattice_at(
  const float2 coord, const float rotate = 1.0, const int seed = 0) {
  const auto cell(hexCellAt(coord));
  return hex_lattice(
           tiles: hex_tile[3](
             hexTileOf(cell, 0, rotate, seed),
             hexTileOf(cell, 1, rotate, seed),
             hexTileOf(cell, 2, rotate, seed)),
           weights: hexSharpen(cell.barycentric));
}

/// The kernel of `hex_tile_at()`, with the random number explicit.
@(pure noinline)
hex_tile hexTileAt(
  const float2 coord,
  const float rotate,
  const int seed,
  const float contrast,
  const float xi) {
  const auto cell(hexCellAt(coord));
  const auto w(weight_contrast(hexSharpen(cell.barycentric), contrast));
  return hexTileOf(cell, weight_select(w, xi), rotate, seed);
}

/// One hex tile covering `coord`, chosen with probability equal to its
/// weight: the stochastic path, and the one to prefer.
///
/// The whole cost of the technique collapses onto one tile. The barycentric
/// weights are cheap and need neither a hash nor a rotation, so they are
/// sharpened and selected first and exactly one tile is built afterward,
/// which is a third of the arithmetic; every map is then read with an
/// ordinary `tex::lookup_*()` at `tile.coord`, which is a third of the
/// fetches. Averaged over the samples a Monte Carlo renderer already takes
/// this reproduces `hex_blend()`, and because every sample is a value that
/// genuinely occurs in the source it preserves the histogram exactly, so
/// nothing here needs `hex_blend_variance_preserving()`.
///
/// The `contrast` may be pushed harder here than on the deterministic path,
/// because a sharp weight ramp is dithered by the sampler instead of showing
/// up as a hexagonal seam. What is unavailable is the content modulation of
/// `weight_modulate()`, which needs all three values.
///
/// `xi` must be a canonical random number in `[0, 1)`, and defaults to
/// `state::random_float()`, which the renderer is expected to seed per
/// shading point. Pass it explicitly to chain one number through several
/// choices, as `weight_select_remap()` produces, or to keep the material
/// deterministic. See `hex_lattice_at()` for `rotate` and `seed`.
@(macro)
export hex_tile hex_tile_at(
  const float2 coord,
  const float rotate = 1.0,
  const int seed = 0,
  const float contrast = 0.75,
  const float xi = state::random_float(),
) = hexTileAt(coord, rotate, seed, contrast, xi);

/// Undoes the per-tile rotation on a directional value looked up from a tile:
/// the lookup coordinate is rotated by `R`, so the pattern on the surface is
/// the source rotated by the inverse, and a direction read from the source
/// comes back through the transpose of `R`.
@(pure macro)
export float2 hex_rotate_inverse(const float2 rotation, const float2 direction) {
  return float2(rotation.x * direction.x + rotation.y * direction.y, //
         rotation.x * direction.y - rotation.y * direction.x);
}

/// Looks up a tangent-space normal from one tile, decoded from the usual
/// `0.5 * normal + 0.5` encoding, counter-rotated, and renormalized.
///
/// The XY components come back through the inverse of the per-tile rotation;
/// without this the shading normals swim against the visible pattern whenever
/// `rotate` is non-zero. Pass the tile from `hex_tile_at()`, so that every
/// sample is a normal that genuinely occurs in the source and the color read
/// beside it comes from the same tile.
@(pure)
export float3 hex_lookup_normal(const texture_2d tex, const hex_tile tile) {
  const auto n(2.0 * tex::lookup_float3(tex, tile.coord) - 1.0);
  return math::normalize(float3(hex_rotate_inverse(tile.rotation, n.xy), n.z));
}

//--}

//--{ The deterministic three-tap path

/// Three tile samples of a texture, with the blending weights that go with them.
export struct hex_samples {
  /// The value looked up from each of the three tiles.
  float4[3] values = float4[3](float4(0.0), float4(0.0), float4(0.0));

  /// The blending weights, which are non-negative and sum to one.
  float3 weights = float3(1.0 / 3.0);
};

/// The values of `f` at each of the three tiles: the general form of the
/// deterministic path, hex tiling any per-tile computation rather than only
/// a `texture_2d`. The lambda receives the `hex_tile`, so it may read one or
/// several maps consistently at `tile.coord`, synthesize a procedural source
/// decorrelated by `tile.id`, or counter-rotate directional values through
/// `tile.rotation`. Shape `tiling.weights` with `weight_modulate()` and
/// `weight_contrast()` as usual, then combine with `weight_blend()`:
///
///     const auto tiling(hex_lattice_at(coord));
///     const auto values(hex_lookup_with(tiling, \(const hex_tile tile) =
///         my_pattern(tile.coord, seed: tile.id)));
///     const auto value(weight_blend(
///         weight_contrast(tiling.weights, 0.75), values));
///
@(macro)
export auto hex_lookup_with(const hex_lattice tiling, const auto f) =
  auto[3](f(tiling.tiles[0]), f(tiling.tiles[1]), f(tiling.tiles[2]));

/// The luminance of three RGB values, being the usual significance metric
/// for color. See `weight_modulate()`.
@(pure macro)
export float3 hex_metric_luminance(
  const float3 value0, const float3 value1, const float3 value2) {
  return float3(
           math::luminance(value0),
           math::luminance(value1),
           math::luminance(value2));
}

/// Looks up `tex` once per hex tile and shapes the blending weights, leaving
/// the blend itself to the caller: take the result to `hex_blend()` for the
/// published method, or to `hex_blend_variance_preserving()` to recover the
/// lost contrast.
///
/// The wrap mode is necessarily `wrap_repeat`, as the whole construction
/// assumes the source tiles. Values are returned as `float4` and blended as
/// `float4`, so a material converts to `color` once at the end rather than
/// three times: the conversion costs several times what the fetch does.
@(pure)
export hex_samples hex_lookup(
  const texture_2d tex,
  const hex_lattice tiling,    ///< The tiling, from `hex_lattice_at()`.
  const float contrast = 0.75, ///< The S-curve ramp on the weights. See `weight_contrast()`.
  const float strength = 0.6,  ///< The extent of the luminance modulation. See `weight_modulate()`.
) {
  const auto values(hex_lookup_with(
    tiling, \(const hex_tile tile) = tex::lookup_float4(tex, tile.coord)));
  const auto weights(weight_modulate(
    tiling.weights,
    hex_metric_luminance(values[0].xyz, values[1].xyz, values[2].xyz),
    strength));
  return hex_samples(values: values, weights: weight_contrast(weights, contrast));
}

/// The blend of the three tile samples.
@(pure macro)
export float4 hex_blend(const hex_samples this) =
  weight_blend(this.weights, this.values);

/// The weighted blend of three values, rescaled to preserve the variance of
/// the source about a known `mean`.
///
/// A weighted average of independent samples has `sum(w * w)` times the
/// variance of the source, and that shortfall is exactly the contrast loss
/// that histogram preservation exists to avoid, so the deviation from the
/// mean is divided by the square root of that factor: the variance is
/// restored exactly and the mean is untouched. The `mean` is the one thing
/// this cannot work out for itself; for a texture it is the average of all
/// texels.
///
/// This belongs to the lattice and not to the weight vocabulary on purpose.
/// Every point of the lattice is a three-way transition, so the contrast
/// loss is everywhere; layer transitions are localized bands where softness
/// is the intent, and each layer's own contrast is already restored inside
/// its own tiling. The stochastic path needs none of this, preserving the
/// histogram exactly by construction.
@(pure macro)
export auto hex_blend_variance_preserving(
  const float3 w, const auto values, const auto mean) {
  #assert(#num(values) == 3, "the lattice blends exactly three values");
  return mean + (w.x * (values[0] - mean) + w.y * (values[1] - mean) + w.z * (values[2] - mean)) //
         * (1.0 / #sqrt(#max(1e-20, #sum(w * w))));
}

/// The variance-preserving blend of the three tile samples about `mean`, which
/// is the average of all texels of the source.
@(pure macro)
export float4 hex_blend_variance_preserving(
  const hex_samples this, const float4 mean) {
  return hex_blend_variance_preserving(this.weights, this.values, mean);
}

//--}
